  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a robot vacuum map my house?",
      "a": "Many use a spinning laser, called lidar, or a camera to measure distances to walls and furniture while wheel sensors track how far they have gone. Software stitches this into a floor map and tracks the robot's place on it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is SLAM?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a robot vacuum avoid falling down stairs?",
      "a": "Infrared cliff sensors under the front shine light at the floor and watch for the reflection. If the reflection vanishes because there is a drop, the robot stops and backs away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why doesn't a robot vacuum fall down the stairs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a robot vacuum find its dock?",
      "a": "The dock sends out infrared signals the robot can detect, and robots with maps also remember where the dock is. It follows the signal home to recharge.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a Roomba go home?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do some robot vacuums bump into things?",
      "a": "Simpler models have no map and rely on the bumper: they bounce off objects and turn in a new direction. That works, but it takes longer and can miss spots.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my robot vacuum move randomly?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are wheel encoders?",
      "a": "Sensors that count tiny turns of each wheel. From the counts, the robot works out how far it has travelled and how much it has turned, which is called odometry.",
      "asks": [
        "What is odometry?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "SLAM stands for simultaneous localisation and mapping.",
    "The iRobot Roomba, launched in 2002, was one of the first popular home robot vacuums."
  ],
